Now let me rewrite reviewSpecClaims.js. Design decisions:

Thread 4 fix (core claim): 
```js
function stripBlockingMarker(text) {
   var t = text.replace(/^\s*\uD83D\uDEA8\s*/, '');
   t = t.replace(/^\s*\**\s*BLOCKING\b\**\s*[:\-–—.)]?\s*/i, '');
   return t;
}
function firstSentence(text) {
   var m = /[^.!?\n]+/.exec(text);
   return m ? m[0] : '';
}
function coreClaimText(body) {
   return firstSentence(stripBlockingMarker(String(body || '')));
}
```

Wait — the test 'AC3: BLOCKING via the word marker only' body: 'BLOCKING: The specification requires X but the PR does Y' → strip → 'The specification requires X but the PR does Y' → first sentence whole → spec claim → demote. Good.

Test: '🚨 BLOCKING: the spec says only *_MAX_TOKENS and *_TOKEN_LIMIT are exempt — this PR exempts more.' → strip marker '**'? No bold here. '🚨 ' stripped, 'BLOCKING:' stripped → good.

Hmm wait, one edge: downgradeBody test body '🚨 BLOCKING\nThe spec says X is forbidden.' — that's for downgradeBody, unaffected.

classifyFindingSeverity body '🚨 BLOCKING: SQL injection via raw string interpolation — user input reaches the DB unescaped. (Also, the spec says prepared statements are required.)' → core claim = 'SQL injection ... unescaped' → no spec claim → blocking kept. New test to add.

But wait — what about bodies where the spec claim IS the first sentence but followed by code defect evidence in later sentences? e.g. 'The spec says X is forbidden. Also the code has SQL injection.' → demoted. The reviewer said "At minimum, demote only when the spec-claim regex matches before any code-level defect evidence". Sentence-level satisfies this minimum for the verified example.

Thread 5 fixes:
```js
var FILE_CITATION_RE = /[A-Za-z0-9_.\-\/]+\.(?:java|kt|kts|js|jsx|ts|tsx|dart|py|go|rs|rb|cs|cpp|c|h|hpp|swift|m|md|markdown|json|ya?ml|xml|gradle|toml|sh|sql|log)(?::\d+(?:-\d+)?|#[LA]?\d+(?:-(?:[LA]?\d+))?)/;
var DOC_URL_RE = /https?:\/([^\s\/)<>\]]+)/;
var DEFAULT_TRUSTED_DOC_HOSTS = ['github.com', 'gitlab.com', 'bitbucket.org'];
```

hasVerifiableCitation(body, opts):
```js
function hasVerifiableCitation(body, opts) {
   var text = String(body || '');
   if (!text) return false;
   if (FILE_CITATION_RE.test(text)) return true;
   var m = DOC_URL_RE.exec(text);
   if (!m) return false;
   return hostIsTrusted(m[1], trustedDocHosts(opts));
}
```
Hmm, but a body could have multiple URLs — first untrusted, second trusted. Loop over all matches:
```js
function hasTrustedDocUrl(text, hosts) {
   var m; var re = new RegExp(DOC_URL_RE.source, 'g');
   while ((m = re.exec(text)) !== null) { if (hostIsTrusted(m[1], hosts)) return true; }
   return false;
}
```
GralJS supports RegExp with g and exec loops. Fine.

hostIsTrusted(host, hosts): lowercase; trusted if host === h or host endsWith('.' + h).

trustedDocHosts(opts): opts may be array or object {trustedDocHosts: [...]}. 
```js
function trustedDocHosts(opts) {
   var list = Array.isArray(opts) ? opts : (opts && opts.trustedDocHosts);
   if (Array.isArray(list) && list.length) return list;
   return DEFAULT_TRUSTED_DOC_HOSTS;
