Wait — there's a subtle issue with resolveCommentBody + applyDemotion: if body is empty string (no file fallback, no readText), resolved.inline = true and applyDemotion sets c.body = newBody. Original code: `if (inlineBody) { c.body = newBody; } else if (fromFile && c.comment) {...}` — with inlineBody === '', original did NOT rewrite anything but DID count downgrade and census. Hmm, original: `out.downgrades++; ... if (inlineBody) {...} else if (fromFile && c.comment) {...}` — census counted via out.downgrades in rebalance regardless. So original: empty body with severity BLOCKING field + spec claim → downgraded, census move, but no body rewrite (marker never in body anyway).

My version: resolved.inline=true for empty string → c.body = newBody — sets body from '' to the demoted note text. That CHANGES posted behavior (a body appears where none existed). Minor, but to preserve parity: in applyDemotion, only rewrite when body non-empty:

```js
if (resolved.inline) {
  if (resolved.body) { c.body = newBody; censusMove = true; } 
```
Hmm but then censusMove false → no census move → parity break in census. Original counted census for this case (downgrades incremented → rebalance used out.downgrades). Let me restructure: census moves once per physical comment demoted. For empty-body inline entries there's no physical comment text but the entry itself is a distinct census item... 

Actually simplest parity-preserving approach: keep censusMove = true for inline always (entry-level census), but only assign c.body when non-empty:

